Both op-amps are ideal and operate with negative feedback. <img src="img/523a12fb38b38773.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,288px)"> The first op-amp is an inverting amplifier with \[R_{in}=10\,\text{k}\Omega,\qquad
R_f=20\,\text{k}\Omega.\] Hence, \[V_{o1}
=-\frac{20}{10}\times1
=-2\,\text{V}.\] The second op-amp is also an inverting amplifier with \[R_{in}=20\,\text{k}\Omega,\qquad
R_f=20\,\text{k}\Omega.\] Therefore, \[V_{o2}
=-\frac{20}{20}(-2)
=2\,\text{V}.\] The source current is the algebraic sum of currents through the two branches: \[I
=\frac{1-0}{10\,\text{k}\Omega}
+\frac{1-2}{10.1\,\text{k}\Omega}.\] Thus, \[I
=100\,\mu\text{A}
-99.01\,\mu\text{A}
=0.99\,\mu\text{A}.\] \[\boxed{0.99}\]

The non-inverting amplifier has a closed-loop gain of \[A_v=15.\] For an input amplitude of \[V_{in}=0.3\,\text{V},\] the output amplitude is \[V_{o}=15\times0.3=4.5\,\text{V}.\] Since the supply rails are \(\pm5\,\text{V}\), the output swing of \(4.5\,\text{V}\) is within the linear range. To avoid slew-rate distortion, \[\text{SR}\geq\left(\frac{dV_o}{dt}\right)_{\max}
=2\pi fV_o.\] Given \[\text{SR}=0.5\times10^6\,\text{V/s},\] we get \[0.5\times10^6
=2\pi f(4.5).\] Hence, \[f=\frac{0.5\times10^6}{2\pi\times4.5}
\approx1.768\times10^4\,\text{Hz}
=17.7\,\text{kHz}.\] \[\boxed{17.7}\]

The right op-amp acts as an ideal Schmitt trigger. Since the two resistors connected to its non-inverting terminal are equal, \[V_{+}=\frac{V_{o}+V_{Z}}{2}\] where the zener network limits \[V_{Z}=\pm 5\,\text{V}.\] <img src="img/e2ba1706c71cc423.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,288px)"> Hence, the switching thresholds are \[V_{T\pm}=\pm 2.5\,\text{V}.\] The first op-amp is an inverting integrator with \[R=25\,\text{k}\Omega,\qquad
C=0.1\,\text{nF}.\] The square-wave output of the Schmitt trigger causes the integrator output to ramp linearly between \[-2.5\,\text{V}\;\text{and}\;+2.5\,\text{V},\] i.e., a voltage change of \[\Delta V=5\,\text{V}.\] The integrator slope is \[\left|\frac{dV}{dt}\right|
=\frac{5}{RC}.\] Therefore, the time for one ramp is \[\frac{T}{2}
=\frac{\Delta V}{dV/dt}
=\frac{5}{5/(RC)}
=RC.\] Thus, \[T=2RC
=2(25\times10^{3})(0.1\times10^{-9})
=5\times10^{-6}\,\text{s}.\] Hence, \[f=\frac{1}{T}
=\frac{1}{5\times10^{-6}}
=2\times10^{5}\,\text{Hz}
=200\,\text{kHz}.\] \[\boxed{200}\]

Since the op-amp is ideal and negative feedback is present, <img src="img/c0f104736c67c698.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,224px)"> \[V_{-}=V_{+}=2\,\text{V}\] Hence, the collector voltage is \[V_{C}=2\,\text{V}\] The current through the resistor is \[I_{R}=\frac{5-2}{10.5\,\text{k}\Omega}
=\frac{3}{10.5\,\text{k}\Omega}
=285.7\,\mu\text{A}\] Since no current goes in opamp, \(I_{R}=I_{C}\) Using \[I_{C}=\beta I_{B}, \qquad \beta=20\] \[I_{E}=I_{C}+\frac{I_{C}}{20}
=I_{C}\left(1+\frac{1}{20}\right)
=\frac{21}{20}I_{C}\] \[I_{E}=I_{0}=285.7\times\frac{21}{20}
=299.985\,\mu\text{A}
\approx 300 \mu\text{A}\]

The loop transfer function is \[G(s)H(s)
=
\frac{s^2+0.2s+100}{2(s^2-0.2s+100)}\] The characteristic equation is \(1+G(s)H(s)=0\) \[1+\frac{s^2+0.2s+100}{2(s^2-0.2s+100)}=0\] \[3s^2-0.2s+300=0\] Applying the Routh-Hurwitz criterion, \[\begin{array}{c|cc}
s^2 &amp; 3 &amp; 300\\
s^1 &amp; -0.2 &amp; 0\\
s^0 &amp; 300 &amp;
